#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096367 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f0ec6b-9cb4-5246-9ac6-23b35e3b224e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector as a Sum or Difference of More Than Two Known Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "e47a8da7-9276-5f4c-8ee6-1fd737c2e9ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-4277" :question/problem "Consider the diagram shown below, where $\\overrightarrow{PQ}=\\mathbf{a}$, $\\overrightarrow{PT}=\\mathbf{b}$, and $\\overrightarrow{QS}=\\mathbf{c}$. Find $\\overrightarrow{TS}$ in terms of $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b}$, and $\\mathbf{c}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1102/Source/Images/ee9aee4946e062b8a7ad1fae2e350d37.png)" :question/worked-solution "We want to find a \"path\" from $T$ to $S$ consisting only of the vectors $\\mathbf a, \\mathbf b$, or $\\mathbf c$.\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\overrightarrow{TS}}} = {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{TP}}} + {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{PQ}}} + {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{QS}}}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1102/Source/Images/70335ddb9f1d79ca14b9138a4291465d.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\overrightarrow{TP}} = -\\mathbf{b}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}\\overrightarrow{PQ}} = \\mathbf{a}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}\\overrightarrow{QS}} = \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1102/Source/Images/f5c06f75325a6ca44c7da008f6706fb2.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTS^(→) &= TP^(→) + PQ^(→) + QS^(→) \\\\\n&=-b + a + c \\\\\n&= a - b + c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096409 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c015dcaa-2155-5d80-985b-21924de92b28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Equalities"}] :question/id #uuid "0b0dbbc1-d39d-5aff-9f16-075779d36659" :question/math-academy-id "e-5015" :question/problem "In parallelogram $ABCD$ shown above, which of the following equalities are true?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1102/Source/Images/38d6af513afb9edf4208166303e35ed3.png)\n\n1. $\\overrightarrow{CB}+\\overrightarrow{BA}=\\overrightarrow{CA}$\n2. $\\overrightarrow{CB}-\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\overrightarrow{AC}$\n3. $\\overrightarrow{DC}=\\overrightarrow{BA}$" :question/worked-solution "- Equality I is true. Indeed, $\\overrightarrow{CB}+\\overrightarrow{BA}=\\overrightarrow{CA}$.\n- Equality II is false. Indeed,\n$CB^(→) - AB^(→)|= CB^(→) + BA^(→); = CA^(→); \\ne AC^(→)$\nsince $\\overrightarrow{CA}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ have opposite directions.\n- Equality III is false. Indeed, $\\overrightarrow{DC}\\neq\\overrightarrow{BA}$ since they have opposite directions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096524 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c43200-991b-5854-be1d-fa9db80f3725" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector as a Sum or Difference of Two Known Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "c7526e18-37a9-572a-a6a3-0384bbccf41d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9934" :question/problem "Consider the diagram above, where $\\overrightarrow{PS}=\\mathbf{a}$, $\\overrightarrow{QS}=\\mathbf{b}$, $\\overrightarrow{PT}=\\mathbf{c}$, and $\\overrightarrow{TS}=\\mathbf{d}$. Find $\\overrightarrow{TQ}$ in terms of $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c}$, and $\\mathbf{d}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1102/Source/Images/e1428d48e9b657cb65b4abaf82ef8e51.png)" :question/worked-solution "We want to find a \"path\" from $T$ to $Q$ consisting only of the vectors $\\mathbf a, \\mathbf b, \\mathbf c$, and $\\mathbf d$.\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\overrightarrow{TQ}}} = {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{TS}}} + {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{SQ}}}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1102/Source/Images/d79b0f30be6e0809efe831e4862b1043.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\overrightarrow{TS}} = \\mathbf{d}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{SQ}}} = -\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1102/Source/Images/20b51829636015f084208e464d30bea9.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTQ^(→) &= TS^(→) + SQ^(→) \\\\\n&= d + (-b) \\\\\n&= d - b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c015dcaa-2155-5d80-985b-21924de92b28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Equalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf4bfd85-7d14-53e0-9d3d-15ec4d9b467d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095918} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d6d964-6d81-56b3-97fb-637c0889fe3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095919} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9dc8dd-4eb8-553b-a1dc-7bc86837d9f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095920} {:answer/id #uuid "562e8527-18f3-5dac-81d6-31d71a6be994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095921} {:answer/id #uuid "7c237f98-b098-5b3e-b886-b98e9246bf7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb9dc8dd-4eb8-553b-a1dc-7bc86837d9f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095920} :answer-field/id #uuid "167d3670-cad7-53c9-91aa-cd37b49dc274" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095917}] :question/id #uuid "825f85c5-fa79-52c8-acae-3efa3503365b" :question/math-academy-id "q-69538" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1102/Source/Images/q-69538.png)\n\nIn figure $ABCEF$ shown above, which of the following are equal to the vector $BF^(→)$?\n\n1. $BC^(→) + CE^(→) + EF^(→)$\n2. $FA^(→) - BA^(→)$\n3. $BC^(→) + CF^(→)$"}]}
